<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Pitstop launcher foreground: arc-ticks-redline-needle on Android's
  108x108 dp adaptive-icon canvas. Background is the design token bg ramp
  (#0A0C10) declared in res/values/ic_launcher_background.xml.

  ONE construction rule, in source coords (viewBox 0 0 64 64), shared with
  frontend/public/favicon.svg — the two files must stay identical:

      centre C = (32, 33)     radius R = 24     tick inner radius = 19.5
      angle a measured from 12 o'clock, clockwise positive
      point(a, r) = (32 + r*sin a,  33 - r*cos a)

  EVERY arc endpoint and EVERY tick endpoint is point() evaluated at its
  angle. Nothing here is hand-placed. Regenerating is a five-line script;
  eyeballing a coordinate is how the previous version drifted.

  What that drift looked like, so it is recognisable if it recurs: the
  +95 deg junction where the cool arc meets the redline was 0.61 units
  outside R, which is enough to move the centre an SVG arc actually sweeps
  through. The cool arc ended up centred on (32.50, 33.54) and the redline
  on (32.85, 32.22) — 1.37 units apart, neither on the hub — so the ring
  was visibly non-concentric with the launcher's circle mask (a black
  crescent, thick at the top, thin at the bottom) and the two arcs met at
  a kink. Four of the seven ticks were also off-circle, +-45 by 8.77 units
  and +-105 by 5.01, leaving them floating in the dial face instead of
  meeting the arc. The needle tip sat at r=15.19 against a documented 80%
  of R = 19.20.

  Placement on the 108 dp canvas:

      scale        = 1.35
      translate_x  = 54 - 32 * 1.35 = 10.80
      translate_y  = 54 - 33 * 1.35 =  9.45

  That puts the DIAL centre exactly on the canvas centre. It is deliberately
  geometric, not optically nudged: the mark reads as a circle, so its
  perceived centre is its geometric one, and the ring of background around
  it is what the eye actually measures. The previous 4.18 dp "optical"
  shift was derived from the ink bounding box (32, 29.5) rather than the
  dial, which pushed the dial 0.55 dp low and added to the crescent above.

  Mask fit: furthest ink from the dial centre is R + strokeWidth/2 = 25.75,
  i.e. 34.76 dp scaled, against the circle mask's ~36 dp radius — 1.24 dp
  of margin. Raising the scale eats that margin and starts clipping the
  +-135 deg arc ends.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <group
        android:translateX="10.8"
        android:translateY="9.45"
        android:scaleX="1.35"
        android:scaleY="1.35">

        <!-- Cool body arc: -135 deg to +95 deg, ink-on-bg -->
        <path
            android:strokeColor="#E7E9EE"
            android:strokeAlpha="0.92"
            android:strokeWidth="3.5"
            android:strokeLineCap="butt"
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:pathData="M 15.03 49.97 A 24 24 0 1 1 55.91 35.09" />

        <!-- Redline segment: +95 deg to +135 deg, coral accent -->
        <path
            android:strokeColor="#FF5B3A"
            android:strokeWidth="3.5"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:pathData="M 55.91 35.09 A 24 24 0 0 1 48.97 49.97" />

        <!-- Major ticks: R=24 inward to 19.5, at -135/-105/-45/0/+45/+105/+135.
             The spacing is irregular but symmetric (30/60/45/45/60/30). -->
        <!-- -135 -->
        <path android:strokeColor="#E7E9EE" android:strokeAlpha="0.9"
              android:strokeWidth="1.4" android:strokeLineCap="round"
              android:pathData="M 15.03 49.97 L 18.21 46.79" />
        <!-- -105 -->
        <path android:strokeColor="#E7E9EE" android:strokeAlpha="0.9"
              android:strokeWidth="1.4" android:strokeLineCap="round"
              android:pathData="M 8.82 39.21 L 13.16 38.05" />
        <!-- -45 -->
        <path android:strokeColor="#E7E9EE" android:strokeAlpha="0.9"
              android:strokeWidth="1.4" android:strokeLineCap="round"
              android:pathData="M 15.03 16.03 L 18.21 19.21" />
        <!-- 0 (top) -->
        <path android:strokeColor="#E7E9EE" android:strokeAlpha="0.9"
              android:strokeWidth="1.4" android:strokeLineCap="round"
              android:pathData="M 32 9 L 32 13.5" />
        <!-- +45 -->
        <path android:strokeColor="#E7E9EE" android:strokeAlpha="0.9"
              android:strokeWidth="1.4" android:strokeLineCap="round"
              android:pathData="M 48.97 16.03 L 45.79 19.21" />
        <!-- +105 (inside redline) -->
        <path android:strokeColor="#FF5B3A"
              android:strokeWidth="1.4" android:strokeLineCap="round"
              android:pathData="M 55.18 39.21 L 50.84 38.05" />
        <!-- +135 (inside redline) -->
        <path android:strokeColor="#FF5B3A"
              android:strokeWidth="1.4" android:strokeLineCap="round"
              android:pathData="M 48.97 49.97 L 45.79 46.79" />

        <!--
          Triangular needle: tip at 80% of R (19.2) on the +105 deg
          bearing, so it lands on the inner end of the redline boundary
          tick. Base is +-1.2 PERPENDICULAR to that same bearing through
          the hub — previously the base was square to +117 deg while the
          tip pointed at +106, which skewed the triangle.
        -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#E7E9EE"
            android:pathData="M 50.55 37.97 L 32.31 31.84 L 31.69 34.16 Z" />

        <!-- Hub dot — accent, on the dial centre -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FF5B3A"
            android:pathData="M 32 33 m -2.6 0 a 2.6 2.6 0 1 0 5.2 0 a 2.6 2.6 0 1 0 -5.2 0 z" />
    </group>
</vector>
